# Component pins — the single source of truth for what Vessel builds.
#
# Every build script sources this file, clones/fetches the repo into
# native/<name>, checks out the exact ref, applies patches/<name>/*.patch,
# and builds. Bumping a version here is the entire update procedure:
# change the ref, push, CI rebuilds that component and publishes a new .wcp.
#
# CI watches this file: a change to <NAME>_REF or patches/<name>/ triggers
# exactly the matching workflow, nothing else.
#
# shellcheck disable=SC2034

# --- Translation engines -----------------------------------------------------

# Box64 — x86_64 interpreter/JIT. Ships the SD8EG5 preset that matches Oryon.
BOX64_REPO=https://github.com/ptitSeb/box64.git
BOX64_REF=v0.4.4

# FEX-Emu — primary engine. Built as Windows PE DLLs (arm64ec + wow64),
# loaded inside Wine. Tagged monthly.
FEX_REPO=https://github.com/FEX-Emu/FEX.git
FEX_REF=FEX-2608

# --- Wine --------------------------------------------------------------------

# Upstream Wine, not a fork. ARM64EC is mainline now, which is the whole reason
# this moved: bylaws/wine was the place FEX/ARM64EC integration lived ahead of
# mainline, but its newest branch (upstream-arm64ec) still builds 10.13 from
# August 2025 and nothing there tracks 11.x.
#
# What mainline gives us, checked in the 11.14 tree rather than assumed:
#   - configure knows --enable-archs=arm64ec
#   - ntdll has the real ARM64EC machinery: is_arm64ec(), hybrid image loading,
#     redirect_arm64ec_rva(), a separate arm64ec unix call dispatcher
#   - load_arm64ec_module() reads
#     HKLM\Software\Microsoft\Wow64\amd64 and loads C:\windows\system32\<value>,
#     defaulting to xtajit64.dll. Pointing that value at libarm64ecfex.dll is
#     how FEX plugs in, so the emulator hook is a registry entry, not a patch.
#
# Dropping the fork also dropped a patch: 0001 existed only to fix a shm_open
# call in bylaws' FEX stats hook, and that code is not upstream.
#
# ---
#
# Now a fork again, deliberately: Valve's proton_11.0. Everything above still
# holds — Proton 11.0 is rebased on Wine 11.0, so it carries the same mainline
# ARM64EC machinery, and the FEX hook is still the Wow64\amd64 registry value
# rather than a patch.
#
# What it adds, checked in the tree rather than taken on reputation:
#
#   - **fsync — present, and unusable on this device.** server/fsync.c and
#     dlls/ntdll/unix/fsync.c, gated on WINEFSYNC. Upstream Wine has no esync AND
#     no fsync — its accelerated path is ntsync, which needs a /dev/ntsync driver
#     Android does not expose. So this looked like the only accelerated
#     synchronisation available to us, and it was the main reason for the move.
#
#     It does not run here. Android's seccomp policy for app UIDs does not refuse
#     futex_waitv (arm64 449), it kills the caller with SIGSYS — and Proton probes
#     it by calling it, expecting ENOSYS or EPERM. Measured 2026-08-13: wineserver
#     died at startup and no session could begin. patches/wine/0022 moves the
#     WINEFSYNC check ahead of the probe so that off is a working configuration;
#     SessionEnvironment.kt defaults it to 0. It stays reachable because the block
#     is a device policy, not an architecture limit.
#
#     Note this also means the WINEESYNC=1 Vessel used to set was reaching nobody
#     on either tree.
#   - **61 default DllOverrides** written by loader/wine.inf.in — the VC++
#     runtimes (vcruntime140, msvcp140, ucrtbase, concrt140, the api-ms-win-crt
#     set, atl/msvcr 100..140) all set to "native,builtin". Upstream Wine
#     writes ZERO. A game shipping its own redistributable therefore gets its
#     own, which is what it was built and tested against.
#   - **The emulator keys already point at FEX.** wine.inf.in writes
#     libarm64ecfex.dll and libwow64fex.dll into Wow64\amd64 and Wow64\x86, and
#     load_arm64ec_module()'s built-in default is libarm64ecfex.dll rather than
#     Microsoft's xtajit64.dll. Vessel still seeds those keys, because on
#     upstream Wine they are load-bearing.
#
# The cost of the fork is real too, and is below.
#
# The trade is stated plainly, because it is a real one:
#   - We go BACKWARDS on Wine: 11.14 -> 11.0, giving up fourteen minor
#     releases of upstream fixes.
#   - We inherit Proton's hacks along with its fixes. Its tree carries commits
#     tagged "HACK:" and a whole media-converter subsystem for Steam video.
#
# Measured cost of moving the patch set, not estimated: 14 of our 17 patches
# carry over with byte-identical additions. Only 0007 needed a semantic merge
# (Proton's update_if_table() has a fake_ethernet_adapter() hack our fallback
# now falls through to rather than returning past). 0008's AAudio driver is
# byte-identical to the version verified on device.
#
# 0001 is new and is the one thing this move actually required. Proton 11.0
# predates two upstream commits that moved the audio driver's loops onto system
# threads, and 0008 is written against the interface they introduce — including
# create_unix_thread(), without which it does not compile. See that patch's
# header. Every patch applies with `git apply` at zero fuzz, which is what
# apply_patches gates on.
#
# To go back to upstream Wine, restore the two values below to
# https://gitlab.winehq.org/wine/wine.git / wine-11.14 and drop patches/wine/0001.
WINE_REPO=https://github.com/ValveSoftware/wine.git
WINE_REF=proton_11.0

# --- Graphics ----------------------------------------------------------------

# Mesa/Turnip for Adreno gen8 (a829). NOT in Mesa mainline as of 2026-08:
# gallium gen8 landed in 26.0-devel, Vulkan is still out-of-tree.
# This branch moves fast and has no tags — the build records the exact SHA
# into the .wcp metadata so any build is reproducible after the fact.
MESA_REPO=https://github.com/whitebelyash/mesa-unified.git
MESA_REF=turnip/gen8

# Prefer pinning to a known-good commit over tracking the branch head.
#
# Community reference: StevenMXZ "Turnip Gen8" V33, built from this same
# whitebelyash turnip/gen8 branch plus Mesa upstream. It lists explicit support
# for a840/a830/a829/a825/810 and reports Vulkan 1.4.354. V33 is confirmed
# working on this device, so it is the behaviour our build should match or beat.
#   Release: https://github.com/StevenMXZ/freedreno_turnip-CI/releases (tag v33)
#   Build recipe: StevenMXZ/Adreno-Tools-Drivers @ A8xx, build_turnip.sh
#
# Note: the "50cbd61" shown on that release page is a commit in the *CI* repo
# (the build scripts), not a Mesa commit — it identifies the recipe, not the
# source. The Mesa SHA has to come from the CI run log or from the built
# driver itself:
#   strings libvulkan_freedreno.so | grep -iE 'mesa [0-9]|git-[0-9a-f]{7,}'
#
# Leave empty to build the branch head; set it to freeze a reproducible build.
# Pinned 2026-08-07. The branch head moved under us mid-session (9c51ede5 ->
# 9c475fc3) and both Mesa components silently changed version, which is exactly
# what a moving ref does. 9c475fc3 is the commit Turnip and Zink were both
# verified against. Clear this to track the head again.
MESA_SHA=9c475fc3

# Zink — desktop OpenGL over Vulkan, built from Mesa as an ARM64EC PE
# opengl32.dll (see build/zink.sh for why a native opengl32.dll rather than
# winex11.drv). It is built from the SAME checkout as Turnip above, so it has
# no ref of its own and MESA_REF versions both.
#
# These two exist only as an escape hatch. The Turnip tree is a fork carrying
# out-of-tree freedreno gen8 work and nothing in its CI builds for Windows, so
# it is possible for a rebase there to break the WGL path while Turnip itself
# is fine. Setting ZINK_MESA_REF moves the Zink build to its own checkout at
# native/mesa-zink (upstream Mesa unless ZINK_MESA_REPO says otherwise) and
# leaves Turnip untouched. Also update ref_var in .github/workflows/zink.yml
# if you set it, or CI will keep watching MESA_REF.
#
# Leave both empty to share the Turnip tree, which is the intended state.
ZINK_MESA_REPO=
ZINK_MESA_REF=

# libadrenotools — the only reason a Mesa driver can be loaded on Android at all.
# Turnip builds as an Android Vulkan HAL (one exported symbol, HMI), so the
# platform loader is the only thing that can load it, and it will only load a
# driver out of a vendor namespace. libadrenotools interposes android_dlopen_ext
# so the driver can come from app storage instead. See
# docs/ARCHITECTURE.md#turnip-does-need-adrenotools.
#
# VENDORED, NOT FETCHED — unlike everything else in this file. It ships inside
# the APK rather than as a .wcp, because its hook objects must live in
# `applicationInfo.nativeLibraryDir` and nowhere else works; the reasoning is in
# app/src/main/cpp/adrenotools/README.md. No build script reads these two values
# and there is no ADRENOTOOLS_REF for CI to watch. They are here because this
# file is where the question "what version of X does Vessel build?" is answered,
# and an exception that is not written down is a version nobody can find.
#
# Updating it means re-copying the source into app/src/main/cpp/adrenotools/ and
# changing the commit here and in that directory's README.
ADRENOTOOLS_REPO=https://github.com/bylaws/libadrenotools.git
ADRENOTOOLS_SHA=8fae8ce
ADRENOTOOLS_LINKERNSBYPASS_SHA=aa39758

DXVK_REPO=https://github.com/doitsujin/dxvk.git
DXVK_REF=v2.7.1

VKD3D_REPO=https://github.com/HansKristian-Work/vkd3d-proton.git
VKD3D_REF=v3.0.1

# --- Wine's Android dependencies -----------------------------------------------
#
# The NDK ships no X11 and no FreeType, so build/x11-sysroot.sh cross-builds them
# into a staging sysroot that Wine's unix side links against. These are upstream
# release tarballs, not git refs, so they are pinned by version string; the URLs
# are assembled in x11-sysroot.sh from these values.
#
# Every one of these is the newest release that still ships an autotools
# configure script — verified 2026-08-07 by unpacking each tarball. X.Org is
# migrating to meson, and several of these (xorgproto, libXau, libXfixes,
# libXrandr, libXcomposite, libXxf86vm, libXinerama) already carry a meson.build
# alongside. When one of them drops configure entirely, either pin the last
# autotools release or teach x11-sysroot.sh to use meson for that package;
# the script fails loudly on a missing configure rather than skipping it.
#
# Bumping any value here invalidates the sysroot stamp and rebuilds the stack.
X11_UTIL_MACROS=1.20.2
X11_XORGPROTO=2025.1
X11_XTRANS=1.6.0
X11_LIBXAU=1.0.12
X11_XCBPROTO=1.17.0
X11_PTHREAD_STUBS=0.5
X11_LIBXCB=1.17.0
# libxshmfence is not Wine's dependency — it is Mesa's. The X11 WSI in
# wsi_x11.c uses a shared-memory fence per DRI3 buffer to know when the server
# has finished with it, so `-Dplatforms=x11` will not configure without
# xshmfence.pc. Newest release; still autotools (verified by unpacking).
X11_LIBXSHMFENCE=1.3.3
X11_LIBX11=1.8.13
X11_LIBXEXT=1.3.7
X11_LIBXRENDER=0.9.12
X11_LIBXFIXES=6.0.2
X11_LIBXI=1.8.3
X11_LIBXRANDR=1.5.5
X11_LIBXCURSOR=1.2.3
X11_LIBXCOMPOSITE=0.4.7
X11_LIBXXF86VM=1.1.7
X11_LIBXINERAMA=1.1.6

# FreeType is not X11 but shares the same sysroot: Wine's configure treats a
# missing FreeType as a hard error, and a Wine without it has no glyph
# rasterizer at all.
FREETYPE_VERSION=2.14.3

# --- Toolchain ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Pinned in the Dockerfile, restated here so one file answers "what versions?".
#
# NDK r29 is REQUIRED, and the reason is not the clang version number.
#
# Verified by probe on 2026-08-07:
#   r28c -> clang "19.0.1" (Android build of LLVM snapshot r530567e)
#           --print-supported-cpus tops out at cortex-x4. NO oryon-1.
#           Both -mcpu=oryon-1 and -mtune=oryon-1 are REJECTED.
#   r29  -> clang "21.0.0" (snapshot r563880c). oryon-1 present, both
#           -mcpu=oryon-1 and -mtune=oryon-1 accepted.
#
# oryon-1 landed in upstream LLVM 19, but Android's clang 19 is built from a
# pre-oryon snapshot, so the advertised version number is not a reliable proxy.
# Box64's SD8EG5 preset hardcodes -mtune=oryon-1, so on r28c the build fails at
# compile time. Do not "downgrade to a known-good NDK" without re-probing.
ANDROID_NDK_VERSION=r29
ANDROID_API_LEVEL=36

# llvm-mingw provides the arm64ec-w64-mingw32 target used for FEX's PE DLLs
# and Wine's ARM64EC build.
LLVM_MINGW_VERSION=20250910

# --- Wine's own addons -------------------------------------------------------
# Mono (.NET) and Gecko (HTML), which Wine ships as .msi and installs on first
# boot. **Not optional in practice.** Without them in the package, wineboot has
# nothing local to install from, so a new container puts a dialog in front of the
# user and then downloads 233 MB over the network before it can finish — measured
# on the device, `drive_c/windows/mono` came to exactly that.
#
# The versions are not a choice. `dlls/appwiz.cpl/addons.c` hardcodes the version,
# the architecture and the SHA-256 it will accept, and refuses anything else, so
# these must track that file whenever the Wine base moves. As of proton_11.0:
#
#   addons.c:46  GECKO_VERSION 2.47.4
#   addons.c:59  MONO_VERSION  10.4.1
#
# Mono ships one x86 .msi that serves every architecture (addons.c:60-61).
WINE_MONO_VERSION=10.4.1
WINE_MONO_SHA256=071f4b2887e1c97a11d791ff3d65be9429eed6dec4c2708888bfd546ba358e23

# **Gecko is pinned but not shipped**, and the pins are kept so re-adding it is
# one line in build/wine.sh rather than another archaeology session.
#
# It is 109 MB of package — 55.2 MB for x86 plus 53.9 MB for x86_64, both needed
# because appwiz.cpl is built for i386 as well as arm64ec and each copy asks for
# its own (addons.c:47-52) — and none of it compresses: xz -9 took the mono .msi
# from 85,504,000 to 83,246,320 bytes, 2.6%, an .msi being a CAB that is already
# compressed. Gecko is the embedded MSHTML browser control. Engines that show web
# content bundle CEF instead, and nothing measured here has asked for it:
# Resident Evil Requiem loaded neither addon before it died at the swapchain.
# An app that does want it gets the download prompt it would have got anyway.
WINE_GECKO_VERSION=2.47.4
WINE_GECKO_X86_SHA256=26cecc47706b091908f7f814bddb074c61beb8063318e9efc5a7f789857793d6
WINE_GECKO_X86_64_SHA256=e590b7d988a32d6aa4cf1d8aa3aa3d33766fdd4cf4c89c2dcc2095ecb28d066f

# --- Target ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Selects the flag set in build/targets/<TARGET>.env
TARGET=canoe
